---
export const prerender = true;

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";
import DocRelated from "../../../components/docs/DocRelated.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "Why workspaces", href: "#why", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Worktree or container", href: "#kinds", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Create one", href: "#create", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Targeting a workspace", href: "#targeting", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Warping a session", href: "#warp", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Status and events", href: "#status", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Cleaning up", href: "#cleanup", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Three branches at once", href: "#complete", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Failure modes", href: "#failure-modes", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Parallel work with workspaces"
  subtitle="Run several sessions against the same repository at once, each in its own worktree or container — and move a session between them without losing it."
  description="Guide to nikcli workspaces: worktree and container configurations, creating and targeting them, warping sessions, status and events, and cleanup."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="why" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Why workspaces</h2>
    <p>
      Two sessions editing one directory fight. A workspace gives a session its own checkout, so
      several can work on the same repository at the same time without stepping on each other.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is what makes parallel agent work practical: three branches in flight, each with its own
      files, its own git state and its own session, all on one server.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Not the same 'workspace' as an editor's">
      <p>
        A nikcli workspace is a sandboxed compute environment for a session — a worktree or a
        container — not a multi-root project. The endpoints live under
        <code>/experimental/workspace</code> and the surface is still experimental.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="kinds" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Worktree or container</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>worktree</code></th><th><code>container</code></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Isolation</td><td>Separate directory and branch</td><td>Separate filesystem and process space</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost to create</td><td>Cheap</td><td>Pulls or starts an image</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shares your toolchain</td><td>Yes</td><td>No — the image provides it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Good for</td><td>Parallel branches on trusted work</td><td>Untrusted work, or a different runtime</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extra fields</td><td><code>strategy</code>: <code>git</code> or <code>cow</code></td><td><code>runtime</code>, <code>image</code>, <code>containerName</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>cow</code> is a copy-on-write clone rather than a git worktree, which is faster on large
      repositories where a checkout is the slow part.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="create" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Create one</h2>
    <p>
      The id is yours to choose — it is the handle you will pass everywhere else.
      <code>branch</code> is the branch to check out, or <code>null</code> to stay on the current
      one.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.experimental.workspace.create({
  id: "feature-search",
  branch: "feature/search",
  config: {
    type: "worktree",
    directory: process.cwd(),
    strategy: "git",
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="container">{`await client.experimental.workspace.create({
  id: "untrusted-pr",
  branch: "pr/482",
  config: {
    type: "container",
    directory: process.cwd(),
    runtime: "docker",
    image: "oven/bun:1",
    containerName: "nikcli-pr-482",
  },
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>client.experimental.workspace.adaptor.list()</code> reports which backends this host can
      actually provide — check it before offering container workspaces in a UI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="targeting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Targeting a workspace</h2>
    <p>
      Every endpoint takes a <code>workspace</code> selector, exactly like <code>directory</code>.
      Pass it per call, or bind a whole client to one.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// one call
await client.session.list({ workspace: "feature-search" })

// a client pinned to the workspace
const inWorkspace = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  workspace: "feature-search",
})

const { data: session } = await inWorkspace.session.create({
  title: "search work",
  workspaceID: "feature-search",
})`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="workspace wins over directory">
      <p>
        When both are present the workspace decides, and it replaces the instance directory with the
        workspace's own. A session created with <code>workspaceID</code> stays bound to it, so later
        calls do not have to repeat the selector.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="warp" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Warping a session</h2>
    <p>
      Warp moves a session into another workspace, optionally carrying its uncommitted changes.
      Passing <code>null</code> as the id warps it back to the host project.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`// move it into a workspace, taking the working tree along
await client.experimental.workspace.warp({
  id: "feature-search",
  sessionID: session.id,
  copyChanges: true,
})

// bring it home
await client.experimental.workspace.warp({
  id: null,
  sessionID: session.id,
  copyChanges: true,
})`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      This is the "I started this in the wrong place" move: a session that began in the main
      checkout and turned into real work can be lifted into isolation without being restarted and
      without losing its transcript.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="copyChanges is not free">
      <p>
        It moves the working tree, so it can take a while on a large diff and it accepts a
        <code>timeoutMs</code>. Without it the session moves and the changes stay behind.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status and events</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`const { data: all } = await client.experimental.workspace.list()
const { data: status } = await client.experimental.workspace.status()
const { data: events } = await client.experimental.workspace.events({ id: "feature-search" })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>status</code> is the one to poll in a dashboard: it reports each workspace's state
      without having to ask each one in turn.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cleanup" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Cleaning up</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts">{`await client.experimental.workspace.restore({ id: "feature-search" })
await client.experimental.workspace.remove({ id: "feature-search" })`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>restore</code> brings a workspace back to a known state;
      <code>session.restore</code> does the same for one session inside it. Remove when the branch
      has landed — a worktree left behind is a checkout that keeps drifting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="complete" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Three branches at once</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="parallel.ts">{`import { createNikcliClient } from "@nikcli-ai/sdk/httpapi"

const client = createNikcliClient({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:4096",
  directory: process.cwd(),
})

const jobs = [
  { id: "fix-login", branch: "fix/login", task: "Fix the login redirect loop." },
  { id: "bump-deps", branch: "chore/deps", task: "Upgrade dependencies one major at a time." },
  { id: "docs-pass", branch: "docs/api", task: "Document the public API surface." },
]

const started = await Promise.all(
  jobs.map(async (job) => {
    await client.experimental.workspace.create({
      id: job.id,
      branch: job.branch,
      config: { type: "worktree", directory: process.cwd(), strategy: "git" },
    })

    const { data: session, error } = await client.session.create({
      title: job.id,
      workspaceID: job.id,
    })
    if (!session) throw error

    // promptAsync so all three run at once instead of in sequence
    await client.session.promptAsync({
      sessionID: session.id,
      parts: [{ type: "text", text: job.task }],
      workspace: job.id,
    })

    return { ...job, sessionID: session.id }
  }),
)

// one feed covers every workspace on the host
const { stream } = await client.global.event()
const done = new Set<string>()

for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type !== "session.idle") continue

  const job = started.find((entry) => entry.sessionID === event.properties.sessionID)
  if (!job) continue

  const { data: diff } = await client.session.diff({
    sessionID: job.sessionID,
    workspace: job.id,
  })
  console.log(\`\\n=== \${job.id} (\${job.branch}) ===\`)
  console.log(diff)

  done.add(job.id)
  if (done.size === started.length) break
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      The two things that make this work: <code>promptAsync</code>, so the three run concurrently
      rather than one after another, and <code>global.event()</code>, which spans every workspace —
      an instance-scoped feed would only see one of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sessions still collide</td><td>Created without <code>workspaceID</code>, so they all landed in the host project.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workspace not found</td><td>The selector names a workspace that was removed, or was never created on this host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Feed shows only one job</td><td>Using <code>event.subscribe()</code> instead of <code>global.event()</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Changes vanish after a warp</td><td><code>copyChanges</code> not set.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warp times out</td><td>A large working tree; raise <code>timeoutMs</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Container workspace never starts</td><td>The runtime is not available — check <code>adaptor.list()</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Branch already checked out</td><td>Another worktree holds it; git allows one checkout per branch.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Workspaces API", href: "/docs/api/workspace", description: "Every workspace endpoint and payload." },
      { title: "Streaming a response", href: "/docs/guides/streaming", description: "The event feed this guide fans out over." },
      { title: "Sessions", href: "/docs/sessions", description: "What a session is and what binds it to a workspace." },
      { title: "Using the API", href: "/docs/guides/api", description: "The client this guide assumes." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
